  "worked_solution": "First, we compute ${U}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{2} & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${U}^{2}=U,$ so $U$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${U}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{3} & ={U}^{2}\\cdot U \\\\  & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & ={U}^{2} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{U}^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
